# What promoting a plain `WHEN`'s condition has to keep.
#
# The condition used to be part of one `Op::WhenTest` doing the whole job:
# evaluate the expression, trace the value, validate it as exactly "0"/"1",
# answer the jump. It is now the expression's own native ops followed by
# `Op::Condition` tagged `WHEN` -- except where `native_shape` declines the
# expression, and then it is still the whole job in one `Op::WhenTest`.
#
# A `WHEN CASE` never had a condition at all: its values are compared against
# the enclosing `SELECT CASE`'s text, and it stays on `Op::WhenTest` whatever
# its values look like. An absorbed `WHEN` -- one that is another `WHEN`'s
# consequence rather than a branch its `SELECT` collected -- stays on
# `Op::Generic`. The last two rows here are those two shapes, and what they
# record is that the bytes did not move.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE, on both
# engines, before this file was written. Regenerate a row by running its
# program under the oracle, never with REWRITE=1 -- ir_dual.rs refuses to run
# at all under REWRITE for that reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty, and
# trailing blanks are part of what is compared: a `WHEN` header's `*-*` echo
# ends in one, because the instruction ends at the THEN keyword.
#
# `/nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex` is `ir_dual.rs`'s own INLINE_PATH, which a
# raised condition's middle line prints. Running the same program under the
# oracle prints the oracle's own path there instead.

# 34.2, the WHEN keyword's own raiser, from a condition that compiled.
# `Op::Condition`'s keyword tag is what picks 34.2 over an `IF`'s 34.1, and
# this row is measured to redden when a `WHEN`'s op is emitted tagged `If`.
# **Not a unique catch, and the difference is the instrument**: the op-stream
# pins and the corpus shape sweep read the tag out of the compiled stream and
# redden under the same mutation, while this is the only row anywhere that
# sees the wrong sub-number in a program's own stderr. The extracted-program
# sweep does not see it at all, which is what says no corpus program raises
# 34.2 from a condition that compiled.
program
trace r
select
  when 'x' then nop
end
----
rc> 222
err>      2 *-* select
err>      3 *-*   when 'x' 
err>        >>>     "x"
err>      3 *-*   when 'x' 
err> Error 34 running /nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex line 3:  Logical value not 0 or 1.
err> Error 34.2:  Value of expression following WHEN keyword must be exactly "0" or "1"; found "x".

# trace i over a call inside a WHEN's condition, in a scan that runs one WHEN
# out before matching the next. The call takes an `Op::CallExpr` addressed at
# `root.L`, which is the descent `Interp::chunk_node_at`'s own `When` arm
# resolves; without that arm the driver reaches `Loud::call_op_off_its_node`
# instead of calling anything. Measured to redden when that arm is removed --
# on stdout, `medium` against nothing at all -- and **not a unique catch**: the
# extracted-program sweep, the branch-shape table and the exempt-set gate all
# redden under it too.
# The intermediate lines and their order are the rest of what is pinned: the
# argument, the function's own `>F>`, the right operand, the operator, and the
# condition's `>>>` last, once per WHEN tested.
program
trace i
zs = 'abcd'
select
  when length(zs) > 9 then say 'long'
  when length(zs) > 3 then say 'medium'
  otherwise say 'short'
end
----
rc> 0
out> medium
err>      2 *-* zs = 'abcd'
err>        >L>   "abcd"
err>        >>>   "abcd"
err>        >=>   ZS <= "abcd"
err>      3 *-* select
err>      4 *-*   when length(zs) > 9 
err>        >V>     ZS => "abcd"
err>        >A>     "abcd"
err>        >F>     LENGTH => "4"
err>        >L>     "9"
err>        >O>     ">" => "0"
err>        >>>     "0"
err>      5 *-*   when length(zs) > 3 
err>        >V>     ZS => "abcd"
err>        >A>     "abcd"
err>        >F>     LENGTH => "4"
err>        >L>     "3"
err>        >O>     ">" => "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      5 *-*     then
err>      5 *-*       say 'medium'
err>        >L>         "medium"
err>        >>>         "medium"

# A `SELECT CASE` under trace i, whose `WHEN`s are `WhenCase` nodes and stay on
# `Op::WhenTest`. What it records is the shape a promoted condition has to
# leave alone -- two `>>>` lines per value tested, the value's own and the
# comparison's outcome, which is `Interp::test_case_when`'s pair rather than a
# condition's single line -- and the `>K>   "CASE"` echo above them.
# Measured to redden when an `Op::Condition` is emitted behind the
# `Op::WhenTest` a declining `WHEN` or a `WhenCase` keeps: a third `>>>` after
# each pair. **Not a unique catch**: the branch-shape table and the op-stream
# pins redden under it too.
program
trace i
select case 1 + 1
  when 1 then say 'one'
  when 2 then say 'two'
  otherwise say 'other'
end
----
rc> 0
out> two
err>      2 *-* select case 1 + 1
err>        >L>   "1"
err>        >L>   "1"
err>        >O>   "+" => "2"
err>        >K>   "CASE" => "2"
err>      3 *-*   when 1 
err>        >L>     "1"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>        >>>     "0"
err>      4 *-*   when 2 
err>        >L>     "2"
err>        >>>     "2"
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      4 *-*     then
err>      4 *-*       say 'two'
err>        >L>         "two"
err>        >>>         "two"

# A transcript, claiming nothing about catching -- no mutation run against this
# file reddened it. An absorbed `WHEN` under
# trace r. The inner `WHEN` is the outer one's consequence rather than a branch
# the `SELECT` collected, so it has no `when_info` entry and compiles to
# `Op::Generic`. Its condition is evaluated and traced, its own consequence
# never runs, and control leaves the `SELECT` without reaching the
# `OTHERWISE` -- the last of which is why `say 'after'` is the only stdout.
program
trace r
select
  when 1 = 1 then when 2 = 2 then say 'inner'
  otherwise say 'other'
end
say 'after'
----
rc> 0
out> after
err>      2 *-* select
err>      3 *-*   when 1 = 1 
err>        >>>     "1"
err>      3 *-*     then
err>      3 *-*       when 2 = 2 
err>        >>>         "1"
err>      6 *-* say 'after'
err>        >>>   "after"
